---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/communication-studies/foundational/communication-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Communication theory asks the field's founding question: what actually happens when people
    communicate? The answers form a spectrum — from engineering's transmission pipes to the
    constitutive claim that our realities are built in communication itself. Knowing where a
    theory sits on that spectrum tells you what it can explain, and what it quietly assumes away.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from pipes to constitutive: the model spectrum" widget="equationStep" config={modelsCfg} />

  <h2>The transmission model's genius and its trap</h2>
  <p>
    Shannon's 1948 model solved a real problem — reliable signalling over noisy channels — and
    gave us bandwidth, encoding and noise as rigorous ideas. Its trap is metaphor creep: human
    communication is not information flowing between containers. Meaning is negotiated,
    misaligned, and made in context — which is why later models moved the field from fidelity to
    interpretation.
  </p>

  <h2>Semiotics: meaning lives in codes</h2>
  <p>
    Signs are two-part: a signifier (sound, mark, image) and a signified (the concept). The link
    is arbitrary and conventional — which is why the same gesture flatters in one culture and
    insults in another, and why advertising works by attaching meanings to signs deliberately.
    Semiotics gives communication research its microscope.
  </p>

  <h2>The medium is an argument</h2>
  <p>
    McLuhan's provocation survives as a research programme: each medium's affordances — its
    persistence, reach, speed, editability — reshape what communication can be. A covenant
    whispered, a letter kept for a century, a post screenshot in seconds: the message survives,
    but the social physics differ. Modern platform studies are this idea with data.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCA learning outcomes; Shannon &amp; Weaver, <em>The Mathematical Theory of Communication</em> (1948); McLuhan, <em>Understanding Media</em> (1964); Craig, "Communication Theory as a Field" (1999).</p>
</TopicPage>
